<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="page5-ultimatum-water-pollution" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>The Ultimatum Minigame and Water Pollution</title>

  <p>
    This page has two more examples of finding mixed strategy Nash
    equilibria with the Fundamental Theorem (Theorem 5.2). In the first one,
    the two players end up using different numbers of active strategies. The
    second one has three players!
  </p>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- Section 5.6: Ultimatum Minigame                              -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <assemblage xml:id="read-5-6">
    <title>Section 5.6, The Ultimatum Minigame</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read all of Section 5.6.</alert>
    </p>
    <p>
      It's a short section. Pay attention to how the book uses the
      Fundamental Theorem: first on the pure strategy equilibrium
      <m>(f, r)</m>, and then on the mixed profiles
      <m>(f,\ qa + (1-q)r)</m>.
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <p>
    Let's make sure the way the book applied the theorem is clear. The book
    looks at strategy profiles of the form <m>(f,\ qa + (1-q)r)</m> with
    <m>0 \lt q \lt 1</m>. Theorem 5.2 gives a list of conditions to check,
    and which conditions show up depends on which strategies are active.
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="check-ultimatum-conditions">
    <statement>
      <p>
        To show that <m>(f,\ qa + (1-q)r)</m> with <m>0 \lt q \lt 1</m> is a
        Nash equilibrium, which conditions from Theorem 5.2 do we need to
        check? Here <m>\sigma_2 = qa + (1-q)r</m> is Bob's mixed strategy.
      </p>
      <p>
        Drag every condition we need into the answer area. The order doesn't
        matter, and not every condition belongs!
      </p>
    </statement>
    <blocks>
      <block name="bob-eq" depends=""><m>\pi_2(f, a) = \pi_2(f, r)</m></block>
      <block name="alice-ineq" depends=""><m>\pi_1(f, \sigma_2) \geq \pi_1(u, \sigma_2)</m></block>
      <block correct="no"><m>\pi_1(f, \sigma_2) = \pi_1(u, \sigma_2)</m></block>
      <block correct="no"><m>\pi_1(u, \sigma_2) \geq \pi_1(f, \sigma_2)</m></block>
      <block correct="no"><m>\pi_2(f, \sigma_2) \geq \pi_2(f, r)</m></block>
    </blocks>
    <hint>
      <p>
        Alice has only one active strategy, <m>f</m>. Both of Bob's pure
        strategies, <m>a</m> and <m>r</m>, are active.
      </p>
    </hint>
    <solution>
      <p>
        <m>\pi_2(f, a) = \pi_2(f, r)</m> and
        <m>\pi_1(f, \sigma_2) \geq \pi_1(u, \sigma_2)</m>. Bob's two
        active strategies must earn him the same payoff (condition (1)), and
        Alice's inactive <m>u</m> can't beat her active <m>f</m> (condition
        (2)). Alice has only one active strategy, so there's no equality for
        her, and Bob has no inactive strategies, so there's no inequality for
        him.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    So the equality <m>\pi_2(f, a) = \pi_2(f, r)</m> holds automatically
    (both are <m>2</m>), and all the work is in Alice's inequality, which
    comes out to <m>q \leq \frac{2}{3}</m>. Before moving on, let's make sure
    that answer makes sense.
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="check-ultimatum-why-upper-bound">
    <statement>
      <p>
        The condition turned out to be <m>q \leq \frac{2}{3}</m>. Why does it
        make sense that this is an upper limit on <m>q</m>, rather than a
        lower limit?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Bob wants to accept unfair offers, so <m>q</m> has to stay in a
            range that keeps Bob happy.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Bob is actually indifferent here: against <m>f</m>, accepting and
            rejecting both give him <m>2</m>. The condition on <m>q</m> comes
            from <em>Alice's</em> inequality, not Bob's.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>q</m> is how often Bob rejects an unfair offer, so he has to
            reject often enough.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            The idea is right, but <m>q</m> is how often Bob
            <em>accepts</em>. The book's last sentence switches to talking
            about rejecting, <m>1 - q \geq \frac{1}{3}</m>, which says the
            same thing as <m>q \leq \frac{2}{3}</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>q</m> is how often Bob accepts an unfair offer. The more he
            accepts, the more tempting the unfair offer is for Alice, so
            <m>q</m> can't be too big or she'd switch to <m>u</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. The fair offer always pays Alice <m>2</m>. The unfair
            offer pays her <m>3</m> when Bob accepts and <m>0</m> when he
            rejects, so it's worth <m>3q</m> on average. Alice sticks with the
            fair offer as long as <m>2 \geq 3q</m>. In other words, Bob
            doesn't have to reject unfair offers for sure. Rejecting them at
            least <m>\frac{1}{3}</m> of the time is enough.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            It's just how the algebra works out, and the direction doesn't
            have a meaning.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            The direction does mean something! Think about what happens to
            Alice's incentive to make an unfair offer as Bob becomes more
            willing to accept it.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    Here the inequality gave us a <em>condition</em> on <m>q</m>. In other
    problems, it can go differently: a candidate profile can satisfy all the
    equalities and still fail the inequality, and then it isn't a Nash
    equilibrium. That's why the inequalities always need to be checked.
  </p>

  <p>
    We're going to skip Section 5.7 (Colonel Blotto). Next up is Section
    5.8.
  </p>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- Section 5.8: Water Pollution 3                               -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <assemblage xml:id="read-5-8">
    <title>Section 5.8, Water Pollution 3</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read all of Section 5.8.</alert>
    </p>
    <p>
      This is the Water Pollution game from Section 3.3, now looking for
      mixed strategy equilibria. Watch for two things:
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            The book writes the payoffs as functions of <m>(x, y, z)</m>, the
            probabilities that each firm purifies. Plugging in <m>1</m> or
            <m>0</m> for one of them means that firm plays a pure strategy.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            The book only writes out the first of the three indifference
            equations in full. We'll do the second one together below.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <p>
    Here's Table 5.2 again for reference. In each triple, the payoffs are
    listed in the order (Firm 1, Firm 2, Firm 3).
  </p>

  <table xml:id="table-water-pollution-firm3-g">
    <title>Water Pollution, Firm 3 purifies (probability <m>z</m>)</title>
    <tabular halign="center">
      <col/>
      <col/>
      <col right="minor"/>
      <col/>
      <col/>
      <row header="yes">
        <cell/>
        <cell/>
        <cell/>
        <cell colspan="2">Firm 2</cell>
      </row>
      <row header="yes">
        <cell/>
        <cell/>
        <cell/>
        <cell><m>y</m></cell>
        <cell><m>{1-y}</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
        <cell/>
        <cell/>
        <cell/>
        <cell><m>g</m></cell>
        <cell><m>b</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>Firm 1</cell>
        <cell><m>x</m></cell>
        <cell><m>g</m></cell>
        <cell>(<m>-1</m>, <m>-1</m>, <m>-1</m>)</cell>
        <cell>(<m>-1</m>, <m>0</m>, <m>-1</m>)</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell/>
        <cell><m>{1-x}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>b</m></cell>
        <cell>(<m>0</m>, <m>-1</m>, <m>-1</m>)</cell>
        <cell>(<m>-3</m>, <m>-3</m>, <m>-4</m>)</cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
  </table>

  <table xml:id="table-water-pollution-firm3-b">
    <title>Water Pollution, Firm 3 pollutes (probability <m>1-z</m>)</title>
    <tabular halign="center">
      <col/>
      <col/>
      <col right="minor"/>
      <col/>
      <col/>
      <row header="yes">
        <cell/>
        <cell/>
        <cell/>
        <cell colspan="2">Firm 2</cell>
      </row>
      <row header="yes">
        <cell/>
        <cell/>
        <cell/>
        <cell><m>y</m></cell>
        <cell><m>{1-y}</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
        <cell/>
        <cell/>
        <cell/>
        <cell><m>g</m></cell>
        <cell><m>b</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>Firm 1</cell>
        <cell><m>x</m></cell>
        <cell><m>g</m></cell>
        <cell>(<m>-1</m>, <m>-1</m>, <m>0</m>)</cell>
        <cell>(<m>-4</m>, <m>-3</m>, <m>-3</m>)</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell/>
        <cell><m>{1-x}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>b</m></cell>
        <cell>(<m>-3</m>, <m>-4</m>, <m>-3</m>)</cell>
        <cell>(<m>-3</m>, <m>-3</m>, <m>-3</m>)</cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
  </table>

  <exercise xml:id="check-water-pollution-notation">
    <statement>
      <p>
        The book explains what <m>\pi_1(1, y, z)</m> means. Now try one it
        doesn't explain: what does <m>\pi_3(x, y, 0)</m> represent?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Firm 3's payoff when Firm 3 purifies for sure, and Firms 1 and 2
            use their mixed strategies.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Close, but check what the <m>0</m> means. <m>z</m> is the
            probability that Firm 3 <em>purifies</em>, so <m>z = 0</m> means
            Firm 3 never purifies.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Firm 3's payoff when Firm 3 pollutes for sure, and Firms 1 and 2
            use their mixed strategies <m>xg + (1-x)b</m> and
            <m>yg + (1-y)b</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. Since <m>z</m> is the probability that Firm 3 purifies,
            <m>z = 0</m> means it plays <m>b</m> for sure. Only Firm 3's
            strategy is fixed; the other two are still mixing. This is the
            same idea as <m>\pi_1(p, 0)</m> in the Battle of the Sexes on the
            last page, where plugging in <m>q = 0</m> meant Bob went to the
            wrestling match for sure.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Firm 3's payoff when all three firms pollute.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Only the third coordinate is fixed at <m>0</m>. Firms 1 and 2 are
            still mixing with probabilities <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Firm 3's payoff when Firm 1 pollutes for sure.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            The position tells you which firm is fixed. The <m>0</m> is in
            the third coordinate, so it's Firm 3's strategy that's fixed.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    The book writes out the first indifference equation,
    <m>\pi_1(1, y, z) = \pi_1(0, y, z)</m>, in full. Let's do the second one,
    <m>\pi_2(x, 1, z) = \pi_2(x, 0, z)</m>, which says Firm 2 gets the same
    payoff from <m>g</m> and <m>b</m>.
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="check-water-pollution-firm2-long-form">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Fill in the coefficients to write out Firm 2's payoffs. Use the
        tables above, and remember that Firm 2's payoff is the
        <em>second</em> entry in each triple.
      </p>
      <p>
        If Firm 2 purifies:
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\pi_2(x, 1, z) =</m>
        <fillin name="g1" answer="-1" width="3"/>
        <m>\cdot xz \;+</m>
        <fillin name="g2" answer="-1" width="3"/>
        <m>\cdot (1-x)z \;+</m>
        <fillin name="g3" answer="-1" width="3"/>
        <m>\cdot x(1-z) \;+</m>
        <fillin name="g4" answer="-4" width="3"/>
        <m>\cdot (1-x)(1-z)</m>
      </p>
      <p>
        If Firm 2 pollutes:
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\pi_2(x, 0, z) =</m>
        <fillin name="b1" answer="0" width="3"/>
        <m>\cdot xz \;+</m>
        <fillin name="b2" answer="-3" width="3"/>
        <m>\cdot (1-x)z \;+</m>
        <fillin name="b3" answer="-3" width="3"/>
        <m>\cdot x(1-z) \;+</m>
        <fillin name="b4" answer="-3" width="3"/>
        <m>\cdot (1-x)(1-z)</m>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate name="g1">
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
        </test>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            The <m>xz</m> term is the cell where Firms 1 and 3 both purify.
            Look in the <q>Firm 3 purifies</q> table, row <m>g</m>, column
            <m>g</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate name="g2">
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp value="0"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              That's the first entry, Firm 1's payoff. Firm 2's payoff is
              the second entry.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            The <m>(1-x)z</m> term is the cell where Firm 1 pollutes and
            Firm 3 purifies.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate name="g3">
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp value="0"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              That's the third entry, Firm 3's payoff. Firm 2's payoff is
              the second entry.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            The <m>x(1-z)</m> term is the cell where Firm 1 purifies and
            Firm 3 pollutes. Look in the <q>Firm 3 pollutes</q> table.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate name="g4">
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp value="-3"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              That's Firm 1's or Firm 3's payoff. Firm 2's payoff is the
              second entry.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            The <m>(1-x)(1-z)</m> term is the cell where Firms 1 and 3 both
            pollute, while Firm 2 purifies.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate name="b1">
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp value="-1"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              That's Firm 1's or Firm 3's payoff. Firm 2's payoff is the
              second entry.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Now Firm 2 pollutes, so use column <m>b</m>. The <m>xz</m> term
            is the cell where Firms 1 and 3 both purify.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate name="b2">
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp value="-4"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              That's the third entry, Firm 3's payoff. Firm 2's payoff is
              the second entry.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Use column <m>b</m> of the <q>Firm 3 purifies</q> table, row
            <m>b</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate name="b3">
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp value="-4"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              That's the first entry, Firm 1's payoff. Firm 2's payoff is
              the second entry.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Use column <m>b</m> of the <q>Firm 3 pollutes</q> table, row
            <m>g</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate name="b4">
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
        </test>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Use column <m>b</m> of the <q>Firm 3 pollutes</q> table, row
            <m>b</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Setting the two payoffs equal gives
        <me>
          -xz - (1-x)z - x(1-z) - 4(1-x)(1-z)
          = 0xz - 3(1-x)z - 3x(1-z) - 3(1-x)(1-z).
        </me>
        Compare this with the book's version of Firm 1's equation. It has
        exactly the same shape, with <m>y</m> replaced by <m>x</m>. That's
        the symmetry of the game at work: from Firm 2's point of view, the
        situation is the same as Firm 1's. That's why this equation
        simplifies to <m>1 - 3x - 3z + 6xz = 0</m>, the first equation in
        (5.13).
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <interactive xml:id="question-form-5-6" iframe="question-form.html?section=5.6" width="100%" aspect="2:1"/>
</section>
